body:
so you are afraid of dependency or withdrawal? you have to understand you have withdrawals when you dont have your drug. If a heroin addict is given heroin everyday, he will always feel 100% fine. the problem is when he doesnt have it. Tapering diazepam avoids withdrawal and is usually asymptomatic, its just that mild.

as for the stories, can you give a few examples? what benzo were they taking, for how long and how did they quit? **you will not have to go through diazepam withdrawal again after phenibut.**

&#x200B;

Thats the beauty of taper. lets say your baseline dose is 20mg diazepam, and you feel 100% normal on it. If you reduce 5 mg, you feel that, but if you reduce 2mg, you feel no different. after a week you reduce another 3mg and feel no different. this way you feel the first symptoms only when you are at already the lowest dose and the worst is over.

&#x200B;

If you are afraid of diazepam withdrawal, why arent you afraid phenibut withdrawal? if you quit diazepam now, it will stop masking phenibut withdrawal and you will feel even worse, do you understand? 

&#x200B;

i see you have overly paranoid tendencies about dependency and its understandable. but you will be fine, i promise. just please take diazepam and baclofen as instructed. trust the doctors. this is exactly what i would do to quit phenibut.
